<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Councils Act 1861</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Councils Act 1861 was a British law that reorganised the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> after the rebellion of 1857. It added non-official members, including some Indians, to the Viceroy's council for legislative business, restored law-making powers to the presidencies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, and gave legal basis to the portfolio system in the executive council. It was the first step towards associating Indians with legislation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>, Sir Syed Ahmed Khan urged the British government to take Indians into the administration. In his pamphlet The Causes of the Indian Revolt he argued that their exclusion from the Legislative Council, which left them no say in policies that touched them directly, was the main cause of the revolt. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, Sir Charles Wood, called the act a great experiment, holding that the old autocratic government could not stand unmodified. It was passed alongside the Indian Civil Service Act 1861 and the Indian High Courts Act 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Executive Council, formerly the Council of Four, was enlarged and became a cabinet run on the portfolio system that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-canning-1st-earl-canning">Lord Canning</a> had introduced in 1859. Its ordinary members, six in time, each took charge of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>: home, revenue, military, law, finance and, after 1874, public works, while the Commander-in-Chief sat with them as an extraordinary member. The Viceroy could overrule the council, as Lord Lytton did in 1879, and in an emergency, when the Legislative Council was not sitting, he could issue ordinances lasting six months.</p><p>The legislative council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> could make laws for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> as a whole, those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> only for the peace and good government of their own presidencies, whose law-making powers had been taken away in 1833. The Governor General could create new provinces for legislative purposes and appoint lieutenant governors, and new councils followed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1862, the North-Western Provinces in 1866 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1897.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1862 Canning nominated three Indians to the Legislative Council: the Raja of Benaras, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Patiala and Sir Dinkar Rao. Their role was small. The council could only advise, could not discuss finance, and its Indian members were nominated rather than elected and drawn from princes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> and nobles. The policy of association begun in 1861 was carried further by the Indian Councils Act 1892 and the Morley-Minto Reforms of 1909, and the whole act was repealed by the Government of India Act 1915, which came into force on 1 January 1916.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian Councils Act 1861” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
